     aria-live="polite"></p>
...
<input type="checkbox" name="candidate_profile_ids[]"
       value="<%= candidate_profile.id %>"
       data-selection-target="checkbox" data-action="selection#update">` },
        after: { file: 'After: candidate_profiles/index', code: `<%= ui_selection min: 2, max: 5, class: "stack stack--lg" do %>
  <%= form_tag merge_candidate_profiles_path, method: :get,
        id: "candidate-merge" do %>
    <%= ui_selection_button "Merge selected", icon: :merge,
          type: :submit %>
  <% end %>
  <%= ui_selection_status %>
...
<%= ui_selection_checkbox name: "candidate_profile_ids[]",
      value: candidate_profile.id, form: "candidate-merge",
      label: "Select #{candidate_profile.full_name} to merge" %>` },
      },
      {
        id: 'show-password',
        title: 'Show password',
        why: 'Four forms marked each of their nine password fields for a controller. A field added without the mark was silently skipped, and without JavaScript the checkbox still showed and did nothing.',
        changed: 'The checkbox finds every password field in its form, so the fields carry nothing. It stays hidden until its script runs, and it hides the passwords again on submit so browsers save them as passwords.',
        before: { file: 'Before: devise/registrations/edit', code: `<%= form_for(resource, ..., html: { class: "form",
      data: { controller: "password-visibility" } }) do |form| %>
  <%= render "shared/vm_field", form: form, field: :password,
        as: :password, input_html: { autocomplete: "new-password",
        data: { password_visibility_target: "input" } } %>
  <%= render "shared/vm_field", form: form,
        field: :password_confirmation, as: :password,
        input_html: { autocomplete: "new-password",
        data: { password_visibility_target: "input" } } %>
  <%= render "devise/shared/show_password", label: "Show passwords" %>` },
        after: { file: 'After: devise/registrations/edit', code: `<%= form_for(resource, ..., html: { class: "form" }) do |form| %>
  <%= render "shared/vm_field", form: form, field: :password,
        as: :password, input_html: { autocomplete: "new-password" } %>
  <%= render "shared/vm_field", form: form,
        field: :password_confirmation, as: :password,
        input_html: { autocomplete: "new-password" } %>
  <%= ui_show_password label: "Show passwords" %>` },
      },
      {
        id: 'rich-text',
        title: 'Rich text',
        why: 'The editor copied its content into a hidden field and needed 20 lines of code to build a stand-in if it failed to load. Two forms rendered their content twice and shared the same ids, and each form wired four to six hooks, plus two per merge tag.',
        changed: 'The field is a real text box that the editor writes into. If the editor can’t load, the text box is already there, labeled, and saving. Merge tags such as <code>{{city}}</code> go in from plain HTML buttons, and turning off Rich text leaves the plain text box.',
        before: { file: 'Before: admin/terms_and_conditions/_form', code: `<div class="rich-text" data-controller="rich-text"
     data-rich-text-serializer-value="semantic" role="group" ...>
  <%= f.hidden_field :content, id: "formatted-content",
        data: { rich_text_target: "input" } %>
  <div id="editor" class="ql-editor" data-rich-text-target="editor"
       aria-labelledby="terms-content-label">
    <%= raw @term.content %>
  </div>
</div>
<%= ui_button label, data: { action: "rich-text#insert",
      rich_text_tag_param: value } %>` },
        after: { file: 'After: admin/terms_and_conditions/_form', code: `<label class="field__label" for="term_content_text">Terms text</label>
<%= ui_rich_text f.field_name(:content), term.content,
      id: "term_content", format: :semantic,
      describedby: "term_content_hint" %>

<%= ui_rich_text "result[value]", value, id: "result_value_editor",
      rich: false do |editor| %>
  <%= editor.field %>
  <%= editor.insert_button "City", "city" %>
<% end %>` },
        plate: {
          title: 'Add action, with the rich text value',
          caption: 'The rule action dialog: the Value field is the editor, with the Rich text switch beside its label and the merge tags under it.',
          slug: 'elements-rich-text-dialog',
          alt: 'The Add action dialog for a rate plan: a Field picker set to Position title with a hint, a Use AI to write the value switch, an AI prompt disclosure, then a Value field with a Rich text switch turned on, a hint about merge tags, a formatting toolbar with text style, bold, italic, underline, strikethrough, quote, link, list, and clear buttons, an editor containing “Hi {{city}}”, an Insert a job field disclosure, an Override the VMS rate checkbox, and Cancel and Add action buttons.',
        },
      },
    ],
  },
];

const results = [
  { label: 'For operators like Nadia', items: ['One inbox for everything that needs mapping', 'Failure reasons in words, on the row', 'Every destructive action confirms first', 'Light, dark, and phone layouts'] },
  { label: 'For the team', items: ['One component library and one style guide', 'Checks that fail the build on drift', 'Bootstrap, jQuery, and page-level scripts removed', 'Widgets as HTML elements, 31 controllers down to 8', 'About 13,000 fewer lines of view code'] },
  { label: 'For investors like Graham and the business', items: ['A demo that matches vmspark.com', 'Fewer ways to reprocess an account by accident', 'Routes, params, and integrations unchanged', 'Two security holes closed'] },
];

const edges = [
  {
    icon: 'ph:users-three',
    title: 'Not yet watched in real use',
    body: 'Both scores come from the code and the reviews came from stakeholders. I haven’t watched operators like Nadia work in the new interface, so I can’t claim it’s faster.',
  },
  {
    icon: 'ph:wifi-slash',
    title: 'Silent save failures',
    body: 'When a save fails on the server, most inline actions show nothing. One shared listener that says “Couldn’t save. Try again.” would raise the scores for visibility and error recovery.',
  },
  {
    icon: 'ph:arrow-counter-clockwise',
    title: 'Confirmation instead of undo',
    body: 'Every delete asks first, but none can be undone. For mappings, where one change touches thousands of listings, undo is the next step.',
  },
  {
    icon: 'ph:device-mobile',
    title: 'Wide tables on phones',
    body: 'Job listings keeps its full table on small screens and scrolls it sideways. A card view for phones needs a decision on which columns matter most.',
  },
];
---

<ProjectLayout title={title} description={description}>

  <ProjectHero project="vmspark" />

  <CaseReview links={jumpLinks} />

  <Tldr problem={tldr.problem} decision={tldr.decision} evidence={tldr.evidence} />

  <CaseSection id="context" title="Audience">
    <CaseProse>
      <p>
        VMSpark takes job feeds from the vendor management systems hospitals use, maps each
        listing to a staffing agency’s own vocabulary, runs the agency’s rules, and sends it to
        their applicant tracking system. When a value doesn’t map, the job stops before it
        reaches the agency, and nurses don’t see the shift.
      </p>
      <p>
        Operators fix those values, and VMSpark sells the pipeline. I checked each decision
        against the needs of <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">both groups below</strong>.
        A change that helped one group at the other’s expense wasn’t finished.
      </p>
      <p>
        The main audience for this work was venture capitalists and angel investors like
        <a href="#persona-graham" class="hero-link">Graham</a>. The goal was to make the case
        for the business.
      </p>
    </CaseProse>

    <CaseCards items={audiences} />
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection id="research" title="Research" tone="ink" wide>
    <CaseProse>
      <Heading level={3} visual="h2">Requirements gathering</Heading>
      <p>
        The admin app looked unfinished just as VMSpark began showing it to investors. It had
        started as a Bootstrap project and collected mixed styles over time. The team knew it.
        They paid for a design and usability audit first, agreed with its findings, and hired me
        to do the work. At their request, we managed the project in Monday.com.
      </p>
      <p>
        We had no opportunity to research VMSpark’s customers, so the evidence had to come from
        the app and from established best practices. I audited about 350 templates, then scored
        the whole app against Nielsen’s ten heuristics, with a file and line for every finding.
      </p>
      <p>
        After the redesign, an AI agent that hadn’t seen my notes re-scored the app from the
        code with the same rubric. It had only the original audit and the repo, and it had to
        verify each fix in the code.
      </p>
    </CaseProse>

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-x-8 gap-y-8 py-8 sm:grid-cols-2 md:gap-x-24">
      <Metric value="3.5 → 7.5" label="average score out of 10" caption="before and after, same rubric, scored from the code" />
      <Metric value="2 → 8" label="error prevention" caption="the lowest score before the redesign" />
    </div>

    <HeuristicScores items={heuristics} caption="Before → after, the change that moved it, and what still keeps it from a 10. The design decisions below explain the changes." />

    <CaseProse class="mt-12">
      <p>
        The audit also turned up a SQL injection in a search allowlist and feed text rendered
        unescaped in popovers and emails. Both were fixed before any styling work.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most of the low scores had one cause. <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">The
        information an operator needed was available only on hover.</strong> The app’s old style
        guide said not to hide required information behind a tooltip. The app did so about a
        hundred times.
      </p>
    </CaseProse>

    <Fragment slot="media">
      <div class="case-band mt-16">
        <Container width="wide">
          <CasePersonas items={vmsparkPersonas} />
        </Container>
      </div>

      <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
        <CaseJourney title="Investor demo journey map" stages={vmsparkJourney} />
      </Container>
    </Fragment>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection id="decisions" title="A design system built from scratch">
    <CaseFor project="vmspark" personas={["graham","nadia"]} stage="tour" />
      <CaseProse>
        <p>
          Re-theming Bootstrap would have kept five ways to render a form. I replaced it with a
          small library in plain CSS. Each component has one partial or helper, one CSS block, and
          one live entry in the style guide. Pages can use only what the library contains.
        </p>
      </CaseProse>

      <CaseCards label="Foundations" items={foundations} />

      <CaseCards label="Token tiers" items={tiers} />

      <CaseProse class="mt-12">
        <p>
          Dark mode is a second mapping of the semantic tier. Matching the marketing site took
          changes to a few semantic tokens, which updated every screen.
        </p>
        <p>
          I didn’t design or work on the marketing site. It already existed, and I borrowed its style.
          Investors like Graham see the marketing site first and the app second, so they should feel like one
          company. The app borrows the site’s navy, its blue, the Spark bolt, and its two-tone line
          drawings.
        </p>
      </CaseProse>

      <Fragment slot="media">
        <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
          <ScrollShot
            src="/images/projects/vmspark/styleguide-tokens-light.webp"
            alt="The style guide’s Foundations section: the three token tiers explained with an example of each, then the full primitive palette of slate, navy, blue, orange, green, amber, and red swatches, each labeled with its token name and hex value."
            label="/admin/ui_playground"
            kind="Style guide"
            caption="Each primitive is named by lightness. A darker step always has a higher number."
          />
        </Container>
        <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
          <Plate
            title="Sign in"
          caption="The first screen borrows the marketing site’s navy and its feeds-to-ATS drawing."
          src={shot('desktop-signin-light')}
          alt="The sign-in page in light mode: a navy brand panel on the left with the VMS Spark logo, the line “VMS to ATS workflow”, a sentence saying jobs from every VMS are cleaned up and sent to your ATS, and a line drawing of three feed cards flowing through a lightning bolt into a stack of cards. On the right, a Sign in card with email and password fields, Show password, Forgot your password, Keep me signed in, and a Sign in button."
          />
        </Container>
      </Fragment>
    </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection id="decision-inbox" title="One inbox for work that needs mapping">
    <CaseFor project="vmspark" personas={["nadia","graham"]} stage="failure" />
    <CaseProse>
      <p>
        The old navigation listed 14 mapping pages with a count on each, and operators opened
        them one by one to find work. I added <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">Needs
        mapping</strong>, one inbox sorted by how much is waiting, with a next step at the top.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each unmapped value blocks jobs from reaching a customer’s ATS. Clearing this list is
        the most valuable work an operator does for the business.
      </p>
    </CaseProse>

    <Fragment slot="media">
      <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
        <Plate
          title="Needs mapping"
          caption="The header states the page’s purpose, the total, and where to start. Lists with nothing to map stay visible and dimmed."
          src={shot('desktop-needs-mapping-light')}
          alt={pairs[0].alt}
        />
      </Container>

      <CaseProse continued>
        <p>
          Each mapping page uses the same mapping ledger. The value from the feed sits on the left,
          the ATS value on the right, and every row has its own Save button. An Ignore switch sits
          beside it, and a hint says what ignoring does.
        </p>
      </CaseProse>

      <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
        <Plate
          title="Professions"
          caption="Each row pairs a feed value with its ATS value. A hint above the rows explains Ignore."
          src={shot('desktop-professions-light')}
          alt="The Professions mapping page: “47 professions need a value in your ATS. Pick one and save each row.”, Unmapped 47 and Mapped tabs, a search field with a Search button, a hint explaining what Ignore and new values do, then rows pairing a profession title from the VMS feeds with a profession picker, an optional new ATS value, an Ignore switch, a Save button, and a delete button."
        />
      </Container>

      <CaseProse continued>
        <p>
          A failed job used to show its reason as a raw Ruby hash in a hover title. Now the reason is
          a sentence on the row.
        </p>
        <p>
          The details drawer opens over the filtered list, so an operator doesn’t lose their place.
          Fields are grouped by where they came from, ending with the raw feed, which is where a bad
          value is usually traced.
        </p>
      </CaseProse>

      <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
        <Plate title="Job details drawer" caption={pairs[1].note} src={shot('desktop-inspector-light')} alt={pairs[1].alt} />
      </Container>
    </Fragment>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection id="decision-safety" title="Confirmations that say what will happen">
    <CaseFor project="vmspark" personas={["nadia","graham"]} stage="risk" />
    <CaseProse>
      <p>
        Deleting a mapping can unmap thousands of listings. Reprocessing an account queues work
        for every open job in it. A misclick costs the operator time to repair and costs the
        business compute and support time.
      </p>
    </CaseProse>

    <CaseCards items={safety} />

    <CaseProse class="mt-12">
      <p>
        Forms follow the same idea: label above, hint under the label, and the error under the
        field it belongs to, linked for screen readers. The heading over a failed form says how
        many fields to fix, in plain words.
      </p>
    </CaseProse>

    <Fragment slot="media">
      <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
        <Plate
          title="Reprocess all, light"
          caption="The title asks the question. The body states the consequence, and the button repeats the verb. Every confirmation in the app uses this dialog."
          src={shot('desktop-confirm-reprocess-light')}
          alt="The confirm dialog over the job listings page, titled “Reprocess every open job listing in this account?”, with the body “Mapping and rules run again on each one and changes sync to your ATS. This runs in the background and can take several minutes.” and Cancel and Reprocess buttons."
        />
      </Container>
    </Fragment>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection id="decision-themes" title="Light, dark, and phone layouts">
    <CaseFor project="vmspark" personas={["graham","nadia"]} stage="signin" />
    <CaseProse>
      <p>
        Operators keep this app open through a full shift, often beside other dark tools. The
        theme follows their system setting, and each person can override it from the account
        menu. A small script applies the choice before first paint, so pages never flash.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each semantic token has its own dark value. Brand blue is lighter in dark mode so links
        and buttons stay readable on navy. A script checks
        all <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">178 text and control pairings</strong> in both
        themes against WCAG AA.
      </p>
    </CaseProse>

    <Fragment slot="media">
      <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
        <Plate
          title="Needs mapping, dark"
          caption="The same page as above, with each token mapped for dark."
          src={shot('desktop-needs-mapping-dark')}
          alt={`${pairs[0].alt} Shown in dark mode.`}
        />
      </Container>

      <CaseProse continued>
        <p>
          Most mapping happens at a desk, but failed-listing alerts arrive by email, and an
          operator should be able to check one from a phone. The main screens were checked at 390
          pixels wide with no sideways page scroll. Layouts collapse to one column below 640
          pixels. Every target is at least 24 pixels, and primary actions are at least 40.
        </p>
        <p>
          Drag-to-reorder doesn’t work well with a thumb or a keyboard, so rules and rate plans
          also have up and down buttons.
        </p>
      </CaseProse>

      <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
        <ul class="stage-grid stage-grid--phones grid grid-cols-2 gap-y-10">
          {phones.map((p) => (
            <li>
              <Plate size="half" title={p.title} caption={p.note} src={shot(p.slug)} alt={p.alt} />
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </Container>
    </Fragment>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection id="decision-elements" title="Widgets that work the same on every page">
    <CaseFor project="vmspark" personas={["nadia","graham"]} stage="tour" />
    <CaseProse>
      <p>
        The design system gave every component one look, but its behavior was still wired by
        hand in each view. Thirty-one Stimulus controllers and a shared script held it together,
        and every view had to repeat the right data attributes to use them. So the same fact
        rendered differently from page to page. “Inactive” was red on the accounts pages and
        grey everywhere else. Only one of five copy buttons told screen reader users that
        anything was copied, and the phone menu let the keyboard wander into the page behind it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each widget became one shared element that every view uses. Each behavior is now
        written once, tested in a browser, and checked by lint, instead of being rewired in
        every view. A screen added later gets the same keyboard support, focus handling, and
        announcements as the existing ones, without anyone remembering to add them.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="#process-widgets" class="hero-link">Engineering</a> walks through each widget with its before
        and after code.
      </p>
    </CaseProse>

    <CaseCards label="Who it helped" items={elementBenefits} />
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection id="process" title="Engineering">
    <CaseProse>
      <p>
        Routes, params, form field names, and HTMX targets stayed the same, so the pipeline and
        every integration were untouched.
      </p>
    </CaseProse>

    <CaseProse class="mt-12">
      <Heading level={3} visual="h2" id="process-widgets" class="scroll-mt-32">Widgets as custom elements</Heading>
      <p>
        I moved each widget into a <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">small custom HTML
        element</strong>, such as <code>&lt;vm-copy&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;vm-dialog&gt;</code>.
        The behavior is plain web components: thirteen elements are registered with
        <code>customElements.define</code>, each a short class that adds its listeners when it
        connects. They don’t draw their own insides, though. The button, icon, and label inside
        each one are ordinary HTML that Rails sends with the page, so the design system’s CSS
        styles them, they show up before any script runs, and HTMX can swap them. Four, such as
        <code>&lt;vm-status&gt;</code>, need no script at all, so the tag is only a styling hook.
        Where the browser already had the feature, I used it instead of writing one.
      </p>
      <p>
        A Rails helper writes each element’s markup, so a view calls
        <code>ui_copy_button "Copy payload", for: "ats-payload-json"</code> instead of repeating the button inside it.
        I chose elements over keeping the CSS classes and only adding helpers, because a tag gives
        every widget the same short attribute vocabulary, in the views, in the tests, and in the
        style guide.
      </p>
      <p>
        We considered ViewComponents for the server side and chose plain Rails helpers instead.
        The VMSpark team preferred them because calling a helper method in ERB, such as
        <code>ui_status "Active", state: :on</code>, was easier to remember than how to render a
        ViewComponent.
      </p>
      <p>
        I made the changes one widget at a time. For each one I recorded the code as it stood,
        moved every screen that used it, deleted the old code, added a style guide entry and a
        lint rule, and tested it in a browser. The before and after code below comes from that
        log. Each group below shows one widget in full, with the Rails helper that renders the
        after code, and lists the others that changed the same way.
      </p>
    </CaseProse>

    <CaseCards label="Why custom elements" items={elementReasons} />

    <Fragment slot="media">
      {elementGroups.map((group) => {
        const [shown, ...rest] = [
          ...group.examples.filter((ex) => featuredExamples.has(ex.id)),
          ...group.examples.filter((ex) => !featuredExamples.has(ex.id)),
        ];
        return (
          <>
            <CaseProse continued>
              <Heading level={3} visual="h2">{group.title}</Heading>
              <p>{group.intro}</p>
              <Heading level={4} visual="h3" id={`element-${shown.id}`}>{shown.title}</Heading>
              <p><strong class="text-ink font-semibold">Why it changed.</strong> <Fragment set:html={shown.why} /></p>
              <p><strong class="text-ink font-semibold">What changed.</strong> <Fragment set:html={shown.changed} /></p>
            </CaseProse>
            <Container width="wide" class="mt-8">
              <div class="code-grid">
                <CodeBlock filename={shown.before.file} lang="erb" code={shown.before.code} />
                <CodeBlock filename={shown.after.file} lang="erb" code={shown.after.code} />
              </div>
              {elementHelpers[shown.id]?.map((h) => (
                <CodeBlock class="code-follow" filename={h.file} lang={h.lang} code={h.code} />
              ))}
            </Container>
            {shown.plate && (
              <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
                <Plate title={shown.plate.title} caption={shown.plate.caption} src={shot(shown.plate.slug)} alt={shown.plate.alt} />
              </Container>
            )}
            {rest.length > 0 && (
              <CaseProse continued>
                <Heading level={4} visual="h3">The same change, elsewhere</Heading>
                {rest.map((ex) => (
                  <p id={`element-${ex.id}`}><strong class="text-ink font-semibold">{ex.title}.</strong> <Fragment set:html={ex.why} /></p>
                ))}
              </CaseProse>
            )}
            {rest.filter((ex) => ex.plate && listedPlates.has(ex.id)).map((ex) => (
              <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
                <Plate title={ex.plate!.title} caption={ex.plate!.caption} src={shot(ex.plate!.slug)} alt={ex.plate!.alt} />
              </Container>
            ))}
          </>
        );
      })}

      <CaseProse continued>
        <Heading level={3} visual="h2">What it added up to</Heading>
        <p>
          Across 18 changes, including the command palette, the job details drawer, and the
          panels not shown here, the Stimulus controllers went from 31 to 8, and 13 elements
          took their place. The JavaScript barely shrank, from 2,475 lines to 2,244, and some
          elements grew: tabs and toasts each doubled, because they now handle cases the old
          code missed.
        </p>
      </CaseProse>

      <CaseProse continued>
        <Heading level={3} visual="h2">The style guide</Heading>
        <p>
          The style guide lives in the app. It renders every component live with the code to copy.
          It also states the system’s rules, including where the system lives, how it changes, and
          which checks it has to pass.
        </p>
        <p>
          It also has a section for AI coding agents. The team builds with them, so the same rules
          sit in the repo’s agent instructions too. An agent asked to
          build a page reads the system before it writes a class.
        </p>
        <CaseCards label="Drift checks" items={checks} />
      </CaseProse>

      <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
        <div class="space-y-16">
          <ScrollShot
            src="/images/projects/vmspark/styleguide-about-light.webp"
            alt="The style guide’s About section: its purpose, five principles (calm over clever, same thing same component, space shows structure, color has a job, real before ideal), governance with where the system lives and the checks that enforce it, the steps for making a change, and a section of instructions for AI coding agents."
            label="About"
            kind="Style guide"
            index="01"
            caption="The About section covers purpose, principles, governance, and instructions for AI agents ahead of the components."
          />
          <ScrollShot
            src="/images/projects/vmspark/styleguide-components-dark.webp"
            alt="The style guide’s Components section in dark mode: buttons, form fields with an inline error, statuses and badges each with an icon, notes and alerts, key-value details, tabs, an empty state with an illustration, and a code sample with a Copy button. Each entry has a Code disclosure."
            label="Components"
            kind="Style guide"
            index="02"
            caption="The style guide follows the app’s theme setting. This capture is in dark mode."
          />
        </div>
      </Container>
    </Fragment>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection id="outcomes" title="Results" tone="ink" wide>
